### Why can you not flip the schema in one deployment?

Fifteen consumers have fifteen independent deployment schedules, on-call rotations, and backlogs. Requiring simultaneous deployment is a distributed lockstep guaranteed to fail.

```java
package com.bank.events;

public class SimultaneousDeployRisk {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] teams = {"fraud", "statements", "rewards", "tax", "audit",
                "notifications", "analytics", "compliance", "risk", "marketing",
                "mobile", "web", "partner-api", "reconciliation", "reporting"};

        System.out.println("Teams that must deploy simultaneously: " + teams.length);
        System.out.println("Team 3 is mid-incident, team 7 is on holiday, team 12 is frozen");
        System.out.println("If even ONE is late → that consumer breaks");
        System.out.println("Possibly fraud detection or audit → unacceptable for a bank");
    }
}
```

```text
Teams that must deploy simultaneously: 15
Team 3 is mid-incident, team 7 is on holiday, team 12 is frozen
If even ONE is late → that consumer breaks
Possibly fraud detection or audit → unacceptable for a bank
```

### How does additive evolution work?

Only add new optional fields, never remove or rename existing ones. Old consumers ignore new fields and keep working.

```java
package com.bank.events;

public class AdditiveEvolution {

    // Original event: all consumers know these fields
    record TransferCompletedV1(String transferId, long amountMinor) {}

    // Additive: NEW fields added, OLD fields KEPT
    record TransferCompletedAdditive(
            String transferId,
            long amountMinor,         // KEPT
            String currency,           // NEW optional
            String settlementRailV2    // NEW optional
    ) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Old consumers: read transferId and amountMinor, ignore new fields");
        System.out.println("New consumers: read all fields including currency and settlementRailV2");
        System.out.println("No coordinated deploy needed. Backward compatible.");
    }
}
```

```text
Old consumers: read transferId and amountMinor, ignore new fields
New consumers: read all fields including currency and settlementRailV2
No coordinated deploy needed. Backward compatible.
```

### How does dual publication work for breaking changes?

Publish both v1 and v2 during a controlled deprecation window. Each team migrates on its own schedule.

```java
package com.bank.events;

public class DualPublication {

    static void publish(String transferId, long amountMinor, String currency) {
        // Publish v1 for old consumers
        System.out.println("Published: transfer.completed.v1 {transferId=" + transferId
                + ", amountMinor=" + amountMinor + "}");

        // Publish v2 for new consumers
        System.out.println("Published: transfer.completed.v2 {transferId=" + transferId
                + ", amountMinor=" + amountMinor + ", currency=" + currency + "}");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        publish("TRF-001", 5000000, "INR");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Old consumers read v1 topic. New consumers read v2 topic.");
        System.out.println("After ALL consumers moved to v2 AND v1 has zero readers:");
        System.out.println("  → Stop publishing v1.");
    }
}
```

```text
Published: transfer.completed.v1 {transferId=TRF-001, amountMinor=5000000}
Published: transfer.completed.v2 {transferId=TRF-001, amountMinor=5000000, currency=INR}

Old consumers read v1 topic. New consumers read v2 topic.
After ALL consumers moved to v2 AND v1 has zero readers:
  → Stop publishing v1.
```

### What role does a schema registry play?

A schema registry enforces compatibility rules, rejecting incompatible changes at publish time before they can break consumers.

```java
package com.bank.events;

public class SchemaRegistryCheck {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Schema Registry compatibility mode: BACKWARD");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Attempt: remove 'amountMinor' field from TransferCompleted");
        System.out.println("Registry response: REJECTED (breaking change, field removal)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Attempt: add optional 'currency' field");
        System.out.println("Registry response: ACCEPTED (additive, backward compatible)");
    }
}
```

```text
Schema Registry compatibility mode: BACKWARD

Attempt: remove 'amountMinor' field from TransferCompleted
Registry response: REJECTED (breaking change, field removal)

Attempt: add optional 'currency' field
Registry response: ACCEPTED (additive, backward compatible)
```

### Why is the schema only half the problem?

Fifteen teams depending on your event means the event is a contract. Changing a contract with fifteen counterparties is an organizational migration.

```java
package com.bank.events;

public class OrganizationalMigration {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Technical half:");
        System.out.println("  Schema registry, additive fields, dual publication");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Organizational half:");
        System.out.println("  1. Discover ALL consumers (including undocumented ones)");
        System.out.println("  2. Publish deprecation timeline");
        System.out.println("  3. Provide migration guide and new schema");
        System.out.println("  4. Track each consumer's migration status");
        System.out.println("  5. Retire v1 ONLY when tracking confirms zero usage");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Failure mode: 'We broke a consumer we did not know existed'");
    }
}
```

```text
Technical half:
  Schema registry, additive fields, dual publication

Organizational half:
  1. Discover ALL consumers (including undocumented ones)
  2. Publish deprecation timeline
  3. Provide migration guide and new schema
  4. Track each consumer's migration status
  5. Retire v1 ONLY when tracking confirms zero usage

Failure mode: 'We broke a consumer we did not know existed'
```

### How do you compare the compatibility strategies?

| Strategy | When to Use | Coordination Needed | Risk |
|---|---|---|---|
| Additive evolution | Most changes (add fields) | None | Lowest |
| Dual publication | Genuinely breaking changes | Per-team migration tracking | Medium |
| Consumers before producer | New form is a superset | All consumers must be ready first | Higher |
| Big-bang flip | Never | Simultaneous deploy of 15 teams | Highest |

### What are common mistakes?

Not discovering undocumented consumers before making the change.

```java
package com.bank.events;

// WRONG: "We told the 15 known consumers. Change went live."
// Consumer #16 (undiscovered): data pipeline team reading the event directly from Kafka
// They break silently, audit data is now wrong

// CORRECT: discover ALL consumers from Kafka consumer group metadata,
// access logs, and schema registry subscriptions before any change
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you discover undocumented consumers of a Kafka topic?
- What is the difference between backward and forward compatibility in Avro?
- How do you track migration progress across fifteen teams?
- When can you safely stop publishing the old event version?
